# The Geometry That Refused to Split

Begin with the metric $ds^2=\operatorname{sech}^2(y)dx^2+\cosh^2(x)dy^2$. Its coordinate rulers pull in opposite directions, but its intrinsic curvature tells one coherent story. For background, see [Riemannian geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemannian_geometry) and [Gaussian curvature](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaussian_curvature).

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## See the global pattern

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact curvature theorem and proof idea</summary>
The curvature is $K=-\cosh^2y+(1-\sinh^2y)/(\cosh^2x\cosh^2y)$. Multiplying by the positive denominator reduces $K\le0$ to $1-\sinh^2y\le\cosh^2x\cosh^4y$. The right side is at least $1$ and the left side at most $1$; simultaneous equality forces $x=y=0$.
</details>

